 WHERE slug = 'openai-codex' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description = $x$OpenAI Codex is OpenAI's coding agent that writes, fixes, refactors and reviews code from the ChatGPT apps, the open-source Codex CLI, an IDE extension and Codex cloud. It is included in ChatGPT plans, with full access starting at Plus for $20 a month.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'openai-codex' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$OpenAI Codex is a software engineering agent rather than an autocomplete tool. You hand it a task, such as tracking down a bug, refactoring a module, adding a feature or writing tests, and it works through the codebase, runs commands and returns changes for you to review. The same agent is available in the ChatGPT desktop, web and mobile apps, in the terminal through the Codex CLI, in an IDE extension and in Codex cloud, all connected through your ChatGPT account.

As of October 2026, OpenAI's Codex pricing page lists five models: GPT-6 Astra, GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6.1 Terra, GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna, with GPT-6.1 Terra added on October 26, 2026 as a cheaper mid-tier option with visual reasoning and computer-use capabilities. GPT-5.5 was retired from Codex for ChatGPT sign-in on October 14, 2026. In June 2026 OpenAI said Codex had more than 5 million weekly active users, and JetBrains research published in August 2026 put Codex's work adoption at 16% in May to July 2026, up from 3% in January.

Codex can run several agents in parallel, use computer and browser tools to check its own work, and keep working on cloud tasks after you close your laptop, with reusable cloud environments for each repository. It can automatically review pull requests, surface findings and answer questions about them. It connects to GitHub, GitLab (beta), Slack and Linear and can be extended with plugins. The Codex CLI is open source under the Apache-2.0 license and installs on macOS, Linux and Windows, and a Codex SDK and the codex exec command support scripting.

Codex is included in ChatGPT plans. Free ($0) and Go ($8 a month) include lightweight Codex use in the desktop app with GPT-6 Luna, subject to rollout. Plus ($20 a month) and Business ($20 per user per month billed annually for 2 or more users, or $25 per user billed monthly) add Codex on the web, in the CLI, the IDE extension and on iOS, with access to GPT-6 Astra, GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6.1 Terra, GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna. Pro costs $100, $200 or $500 a month and currently has no five-hour usage limit; OpenAI doubled Pro's Work and Codex allowances on October 29, 2026. Enterprise and Edu are priced through sales, and GPT-6.1 Sol is off by default there until an admin enables it. Plus, Pro and Business users can buy extra credits, and Codex can also run with an API key at standard API rates.

The main limitation is usage. On Plus and Business, Codex is capped per five-hour window, and the heavier models use the allowance much faster: OpenAI estimates roughly 5 to 45 local messages per five hours with GPT-6 Astra, 15 to 160 with GPT-6.1 Sol, 35 to 285 with GPT-6.1 Terra, 15 to 150 with GPT-6 Sol, and 350 to 3,000 with GPT-6 Luna. Free and Go only get the lightweight Luna model in the desktop app. Codex works only with OpenAI models, and as with any coding agent, its changes and its shell and repository access need careful review and permission settings.

Codex suits developers and teams who already pay for ChatGPT and want one agent across the terminal, the editor and the cloud, and teams that want AI review on every pull request. Developers who prefer Anthropic's models or want to switch between vendors should also look at Claude Code, Cursor or GitHub Copilot.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'openai-codex' AND lang = 'en';

