UPDATE tools SET description = $x$Cursor is an AI-first code editor built on VS Code with agentic coding, multi-repo support, parallel agents, and automated PR review. New customers now sign up through The X Subscription, a bundle that pairs Cursor with Grok from xAI and X Premium, while other third-party models are billed separately at API price.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'cursor' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$Cursor is an AI-first code editor that started as a VS Code fork and has grown into a full agentic coding platform. Instead of just autocompleting lines, it can plan and carry out multi-step changes across a repository, run its own review passes, and hand work off to background agents.

Since October 12, 2026, new Cursor customers are enrolled by default in The X Subscription, a bundle that combines Cursor with Grok from xAI, the Grok bot, and X Premium. Cursor's maker, Anysphere, became a wholly owned subsidiary of SpaceX after an all-stock merger closed on August 14, 2026, which is why Cursor now ships bundled with Grok and X Premium. Grok and Cursor now share a single weekly usage pool for subscribers on this plan. Customers who were already on an older standalone Cursor plan can keep it, but switching to The X Subscription is optional and, once made, cannot be reversed.

Key capabilities include the Composer agent for large edits, cloud agents and cloud dev environments triggered with /in-cloud, automatic code review, and Bugbot for automated PR review. It still supports third-party models, MCP servers, custom rules, and hooks, so you are not locked into any single model family. Recent additions include Projects (beta), self-hosted machines, and Rollouts and Security Review bots for Teams and Enterprise customers.

Under The X Subscription, Cursor is offered in five tiers: Free, Plus at $8 per month, Premium at $30 per month, Super at $100 per month, and Ultra at $200 per month. These tiers draw on a shared weekly usage pool for Grok and Cursor. Third-party models from vendors such as Anthropic or OpenAI are not included in any tier; using them in Cursor is billed separately at API list prices from prepaid credit.

The main limitations are cost unpredictability and the irreversible nature of the switch: once you move to The X Subscription you cannot go back to a standalone Cursor plan, and third-party model usage on top of any tier is billed at API price from prepaid credit, which can be hard to budget for. Being a VS Code fork, it occasionally lags behind upstream extensions, and the most capable features assume you are comfortable letting an agent make sweeping changes.

Cursor suits developers who want an agent that can take on real multi-file tasks, not just suggest snippets, and who are comfortable adopting the bundled Grok/X ecosystem. It is a strong fit for professional teams already living in VS Code, and less compelling for people who only want lightweight autocomplete, work outside VS Code, or want to avoid the X ecosystem's bundled pricing.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'cursor' AND lang = 'en';

